{"authored" [{"question" "q-128340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}$. Evaluate the improper integral:\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty f(x)\\,dx=$$\n\n{{field-1}}" "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/review.json" "file_sha256" "dda068164d341b2543478ad4e3dbaf7cbc1046dd9310731181785ce2726c2bdd" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "11050a6aa2ee06c1219fff44ad88c8f250ed9e86a1143eca625956635755677c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/verification.json" "basis" 1721 "rationale" "The source worked solution begins by splitting a convergent improper integral into two independently divergent improper integrals. Its final answer ln(9/2) is correct. Replace the worked solution with an argument using a common finite R and combine terms before the limit. The imported prompt embeds the same invalid separated-integral equation as earlier-part context; replace that prompt with a standalone definition and integral request. Preserve original source content, correct key, field/answer identities and historical original question."} {"question" "q-128340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "For $R>4$, the partial-fraction identity gives\n\n$$\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx=\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{x-3}-2\\int_4^R\\frac{dx}{2x+1}.$$\n\nBoth terms have the same finite upper bound. Integrating and combining the logarithms before taking a limit yields\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_4^R\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n&=\\left[\\ln(x-3)-\\ln(2x+1)\\right]_4^R\\\\\n&=\\ln\\frac{R-3}{2R+1}+\\ln9\n=\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}.\n\\end{aligned}$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\\int_4^\\infty\\frac{7}{2x^2-5x-3}\\,dx\n=\\lim_{R\\to\\infty}\\ln\\frac{9(R-3)}{2R+1}\n=\\ln\\frac92.$$\n\nThe limit is finite, so the improper integral converges. The two partial-fraction improper integrals each diverge when considered separately; keeping a common finite upper bound avoids an undefined subtraction." "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/review.json" "file_sha256" "dda068164d341b2543478ad4e3dbaf7cbc1046dd9310731181785ce2726c2bdd" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "11050a6aa2ee06c1219fff44ad88c8f250ed9e86a1143eca625956635755677c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/verification.json" "basis" 1721 "rationale" "The source worked solution begins by splitting a convergent improper integral into two independently divergent improper integrals. Its final answer ln(9/2) is correct. Replace the worked solution with an argument using a common finite R and combine terms before the limit. The imported prompt embeds the same invalid separated-integral equation as earlier-part context; replace that prompt with a standalone definition and integral request. Preserve original source content, correct key, field/answer identities and historical original question."} {"question" "q-128340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\ln(9/2)"] "category" "mathematical_correction" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/review.json" "file_sha256" "dda068164d341b2543478ad4e3dbaf7cbc1046dd9310731181785ce2726c2bdd" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "11050a6aa2ee06c1219fff44ad88c8f250ed9e86a1143eca625956635755677c" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/mathematical-corrections/q-128340/verification.json" "basis" 1721 "rationale" "The source worked solution begins by splitting a convergent improper integral into two independently divergent improper integrals. Its final answer ln(9/2) is correct. Replace the worked solution with an argument using a common finite R and combine terms before the limit. The imported prompt embeds the same invalid separated-integral equation as earlier-part context; replace that prompt with a standalone definition and integral request. Preserve original source content, correct key, field/answer identities and historical original question."} {"question" "q-128340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the function $f$ defined by $f(x)=\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}.$\n\nEarlier part 1:\n\nCalculate ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        ${f}^{′}(x)\\,=\\,$ \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{-7(4x-5)}{(2x^2-5x-3)^2}$\n\nEarlier part 2:\n\nThe slope of the tangent line to the graph of $y=f(x)$ at $x=2$ is\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-\\frac{21}{25}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 3:\n\nConsider a function $g(x)$ such that $g(2)=-2$ and ${g}^{′}(2)=\\frac{1}{2}.$ If $h(x)=\\frac{5-25f(x)}{g(x)+3},$ then ${h}^{′}(2)=$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 4:\n\nAt which values of $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3)$ does $f$ have a critical point?\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        The function $f$ has a critical point at $x=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $\\frac{5}{4}$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        .\n\nEarlier part 5:\n\nDetermine the number of local maxima and minima $f(x)$ has on the interval  $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},3).$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $f(x)$ has \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local maxima and \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $0$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        \n$\\,$ local minima.\n\nEarlier part 6:\n\nGiven that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=A{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}+B{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ and $B$ are constants, find $A$ and $B.$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                                        \n                                            \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $A=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $1$                \n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                                \n\n                                                \n\n                                        \n                                        \n                                        \n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                                        \n                                        $B=$\n\n                                    \n                                \n                                    \n                            \n                                        $-2$\n\nCurrent part:\n\n${\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\,$\n\n                            \n                                    \n                            \n                                        {{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2d5e3819c273528c811a3f1ab8f1ccb92bcb5d1d5a02ee2c5573094f8ebc960" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's calculate the indefinite integral first. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int f(x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{x-3}-2\\int \\frac{\\text{d}x}{2x+1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can simplify the result using the laws of logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|x-3|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|2x+1|+C \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the improper integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{4}^{\\infty }\\frac{7}{2{x}^{2}-5x-3}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }{[\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{x-3}{2x+1}|]}_{4}^{a} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{a-3}{2a+1}|-\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1}{9}|) \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡|\\frac{1-\\frac{3}{a}}{2+\\frac{1}{a}}|)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡{9}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡9 \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{9}{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2d5e3819c273528c811a3f1ab8f1ccb92bcb5d1d5a02ee2c5573094f8ebc960" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128340" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "2ce0c9543a727a436f061c6b62155cdd469cd2cb2a713ca1d41d5cfa55fc542f" "basis" 1719 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-128340" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14043928/state.json" "file_sha256" "e2d5e3819c273528c811a3f1ab8f1ccb92bc